  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 419 AM CDT Tue Aug 18 2026\n\nThe Northern Plains lie under a west-northwest cyclonic flow \npattern early this morning between a Hudson Bay low and Desert \nSouthwest ridge. Despite a surface high pressure analyzed over \nsouthern Saskatchewan, enough low level convergence/frontogenesis\nis present to have produced an area of scattered showers along \nthe Missouri River Valley from northeast Montana into northwest \nNorth Dakota. CAMs that show some semblance of this activity \nbring it southeastward through central North Dakota this \nmorning, although prognostics of the low level forcing weaken \nover time. An occasional, brief thunderstorm cannot be ruled \nout, but there does not appear to be enough CAPE to support \nsustained lightning. Aside from these morning showers, a quiet \nweather day is anticipated as surface high pressure slides \nsoutheastward into North Dakota. Today will be a little cooler \nthan yesterday for most, with highs in the lower and mid 70s \nnorth and east to lower 80s southwest. \n\nFrom tonight into Wednesday, mid level shortwave energy is \nforecast to approach the region from the northwest, with a \nsurface trough deepening over western North Dakota by Wednesday \nafternoon. Several CAMs show weak, elevated convection entering \nwestern North Dakota late tonight into Wednesday morning, but \nPoPs remain lower than 20 percent over this time period due to \nsparse coverage and very low RH projected in the sub-cloud \nlayer. But Wednesday afternoon could see an uptick in both \ncoverage and intensity of convection over central North Dakota \nin response to diurnal heating, a pooling of low level moisture \ndownwind of the surface trough, and perhaps a coinciding \nincrease in DCVA. MLCAPE could increase above 1000 J/kg over \nsouth central North Dakota by mid to late Wednesday afternoon \nwith deep layer shear initially only around 25 kts, but \nincreasing over time. Values of 0-3 km shear are actually quite \nsimilar to those in the 0- 6 km layer, which is reflected in \nforecast hodographs that do show a varying degree of low level \ncurvature depending on location. Residual CIN and dry-air \nentrainment in the upper levels could work against sustained \nupdraft growth, but the environment is still forecast to be \nsupportive of a few marginally severe thunderstorms from south \ncentral North Dakota to the James River Valley Wednesday \nafternoon and evening. Shear and deep layer wind vector \norientation to the surface trough favor a mixed storm mode, but \nthe weak nature of the forcing may put a slight tilt towards a \nmore predominant discrete mode.\n\nDespite the passage of the aforementioned shortwave(s) on \nWednesday, as well as another one that could brush northern and \neastern parts of the state on Thursday, broad mid to upper level\nrising height tendencies are in the forecast mid to late week. \nThis brings the high temperature forecast back into the 80s \nWednesday and Thursday, closer to 90 in the southwest quadrant \nof the state on Thursday. Friday may be a few degrees cooler as \nanother shortwave passes by to the north and east. Ensemble \nguidance shows a highly-amplified wave pattern developing \nheading into the weekend, with an upstream ridge being tilted \ninto the Northern Plains and eventually flattened by a southern \nCanadian shortwave early next week. But there is notable timing \nuncertainty in the latter feature, which leaves a near- average \ntemperature forecast in place through the weekend for now. After\nWednesday, rain/thunderstorm chances remain very low until late\nSaturday into Sunday when height falls associated with the \nsouthern Canadian shortwave could begin spreading into the \nregion.",
